# Super Dodge Ball (USA) — "pan" input schedule: idle into a match, then
# alternate LEFT / RIGHT so the camera sweeps back and forth.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Every other vector leaves the camera still, and a still
# camera hides the class of bug that matters most for the widescreen margins.
#
# The game rebuilds its sprite list over two frames and then leaves OAM alone
# for three to six more (measured: the cursor-bump hooks fire on 66 of 200
# match frames). So a margin sprite's screen column is only re-derived when that
# list is rebuilt. If the camera moves in between, the held sprite is at a
# column computed for the OLD camera while the background slides under it. On a
# still camera the two never separate, which is why sdb_idle looks correct and
# a pan does not.
#
# This vector is the reproduction for that, and for the continuity property:
# a player walking into the margin should keep walking outward in even steps,
# and come back the way it went. Compare the margin column frame to frame with
#
#     SDB_TRACE_CAPTURES=<from>-<to> ... --input tas/vectors/sdb_pan.input
#
# which prints the list the compositor drew, one line per frame.
#
# The pan itself starts at frame 500, well inside the match that idle enters at
# ~445, and each direction is held 24 frames: the game's scroll steps 6-7 px per
# frame (docs/research.md 24), so 24 frames is ~150 px, more than half the
# 256-column viewport. That is enough to carry an object clean across the screen
# edge and back, which is the only way the margin is exercised at all.
#
# Format: one "<frame> <BUTTONS>" line per state change, absolute state
# ("-" is release). `#` starts a comment.
0 -
11 START
14 -
21 START+RIGHT
22 -
24 B
27 -
